Six panels, one closer, one of each body variant. The accompanying
copy is placeholder. Real content lands per topic in Inc 7.1 onwards.

{% panels %}

{% panel num="01_SCOPE" eyebrow="WHERE THE LAW APPLIES" headline="If a system holds info about people, [DP applies]." illo="scope" %}
- Booking system :: customer names
- HR portal :: employee records
- School tool :: pupil grades
- Delivery app :: addresses, order history

{% takeaway %}
Almost every digital system stores some information about people. When it does, the DPA is one of the laws an organisation has to follow.
{% /takeaway %}
{% /panel %}

{% panel num="02_DEF" eyebrow="DEFINITION CARD" headline="What [personal data] actually means." illo="regime" %}
{% def %}
**Personal data** is any information that relates to an identified or identifiable living person. A name plus an address counts. So does an IP address tied to an account, a photograph of a face, or a unique reference number that maps back to a real person.
{% /def %}

The bar is "identifiable", not just "identified". If you could work out who it refers to with reasonable effort, the data is personal.

{% takeaway %}
When in doubt, treat it as personal data and apply the safeguards.
{% /takeaway %}
{% /panel %}

{% panel num="03_QUOTE" eyebrow="TERM CARD" headline="The data must be [accurate and complete]." illo="principles" %}
{% term-card %}
"Customer record. No name field. Address half-typed."
{% /term-card %}

Records like this cannot be trusted for any decision about the person they describe. Validation and verification techniques exist to stop them existing.

{% takeaway %}
Garbage in, garbage out. Bad data is a DPA accuracy breach before it is a business problem.
{% /takeaway %}
{% /panel %}

{% panel num="04_LAW" eyebrow="DOCUMENT COMPARE" headline="One [regime], two documents." illo="actors" %}
{% docs-compare %}
{% doc title="UK GDPR" %}
The rulebook. Defines the principles, the lawful bases, and the rights of data subjects.
{% /doc %}
{% doc title="DPA 2018" %}
The UK Act. Implements UK GDPR in domestic law and adds specific UK provisions on top.
{% /doc %}
{% /docs-compare %}

{% takeaway %}
Name both when you write about a personal-data scenario. They function as one regime.
{% /takeaway %}
{% /panel %}

{% panel num="05_RIGHTS" eyebrow="CHIP CLOUD" headline="Data subjects have [rights] over their own data." illo="rights" %}
{% cloud %}
Rights: {% chip %}access{% /chip %} {% chip %}rectify{% /chip %} {% chip %}erase{% /chip %} {% chip %}restrict{% /chip %} {% chip %}portability{% /chip %} {% chip %}object{% /chip %} {% chip %}no auto-decide{% /chip %}
{% /cloud %}

{% takeaway %}
Articles 12 to 22 of UK GDPR enumerate the rights. Examiners reward students who can name the right that bites in a given scenario.
{% /takeaway %}
{% /panel %}

{% panel num="06_FLOW" eyebrow="LIFECYCLE LIST" headline="The DPA covers the [whole life] of the data." illo="lawful-bases" %}
{% def %}
From the moment data is collected to the moment it is deleted, an organisation is the **controller** of that data and is responsible for handling it properly.
{% /def %}

- collect
- store
- use
- share
- analyse
- delete

{% takeaway %}
Each stage carries DPA responsibilities. None of them is exempt.
{% /takeaway %}
{% /panel %}

{% /panels %}

{% closer line="name the law. [then] apply it." body="Working with data protection in any real scenario comes down to two moves, in this order." %}
{% step num="STEP 01" head="Name the law" %}
Identify when personal data is involved, and which Act applies. Usually the DPA and UK GDPR. Sometimes the Equality Act is also relevant.
{% /step %}
{% step num="STEP 02" head="Apply the principle" %}
Explain which right or requirement is in play: consent, accuracy, privacy, security, or the controller's duty of care.
{% /step %}
{% /closer %}
